"[2601.09855] Thinking Long, but Short: Stable Sequential Test-Time Scaling for Large Reasoning Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09855)\nciteturn30938view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09855\",\"lineno\":6}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Jan 2026]\nL9: # Title:Thinking Long, but Short: Stable Sequential Test-Time Scaling for Large Reasoning Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Michael R. Metel , cite6†Yufei Cui , cite7†Boxing Chen , cite8†Prasanna Parthasarathi L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Thinking Long, but Short: Stable Sequential Test-Time Scaling for Large Reasoning Models, by Michael R. Metel and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Sequential test-time scaling is a promising training-free method to improve large reasoning model accuracy, but as currently implemented, significant limitations have been observed. Inducing models to think for longer can increase their accuracy, but as the length of reasoning is further extended, it has also been shown to result in accuracy degradation and model instability.\nL17: This work presents a novel sequential test-time scaling method, Min-Seek, which improves model accuracy significantly over a wide range of induced thoughts, stabilizing the accuracy of sequential scaling, and removing the need for reasoning length fine-tuning. Beyond improving model accuracy over a variety of reasoning tasks, our method is inherently efficient, as only the KV pairs of one additional induced thought are kept in the KV cache during reasoning.\nL18: With a custom KV cache which stores keys without position embeddings, by dynamically encoding them contiguously before each new generated thought, our method can continue to reason well beyond a model's maximum context length, and under mild conditions has linear computational complexity.\nL19: Comments:  | Findings of EACL 2026\nL20: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2601.09855 [cs.AI]\nL22:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2601.09855v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL23:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.09855†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Michael Metel R [cite14†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 14 Jan 2026 20:30:55 UTC (71 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Thinking Long, but Short: Stable Sequential Test-Time Scaling for Large Reasoning Models, by Michael R. Metel and 3 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite9†View PDF L36:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite15†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite16†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.AI\nL43: \nL44: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L45: \nL46: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2026-01 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite22†cs L51: cite23†cs.CL L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.